Shame, really. By the time Nethack 3.4.0 came out in 2002, Angband had
had cross-platform portability of saved game files for some time. But,
Nethack's code base is rich with such relics.

There are compatibility checks for antediluvian Unix implementations
you probably won't find outside of museums or the private collections
of eccentric ubernerds, and which probably won't even run the modern
version at an acceptable speed.

There are conditional-compilation hacks for an overlay mechanism that
probably doesn't work any more and even if it did, it's unlikely anyone
has bothered to check if the overlays are still small enough to fit in
a 16-bit MS-DOS machine's RAM.

In the terms of "cathedral" versus "bazaar" development, Nethack isn't
so much a cathedral as a temple to the machine gods of a more advanced
civilization whose time has passed. People tiptoe through the code,
treating any part whose operation is obscure or erratic as a sacred and
mysterious relic to be left undisturbed, rather than a curiosity - or
nuisance - to be investigated, documented, and if necessary replaced
with something comprehensible and reliable.

When you pass a pointer to an "obj" into the rat's nest of functions
that look at an object and produce a name string for it, you have to
pass a "struct obj *", not a "const struct obj *", because the bloody
thing goes in and starts tampering with the object's internal state -
presumably to avoid having to pass extra parameters.

Not that the coding style would permit a pointer-to-const, since a
the "const" keyword is an ANSI-ism.
